    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Aluminium fencing (aluminum fencing, if you are searching from the US) has become a popular choice for homeowners who want a fence that looks smart, feels secure and needs little upkeep. Unlike timber it does not rot, it looks more refined than chain link, and unlike wrought iron it does not rust. But the appeal of an aluminium fence can fade quickly if the installation runs into common pitfalls.</p>
<p>The unexpected costs of fencing rarely come from the materials themselves. They come from mistakes in planning, legal checks and the way the fence is put in the ground. A single misplaced post or a skipped approval can leave you paying for much of the job twice. Here are the most frequent mistakes and how to avoid them.</p>

<h2 id="boundary-disputes">1. Guessing where the boundary is</h2>
<p>Installing a fence based on an educated guess of where your land ends is one of the most expensive mistakes you can make. Old hedges, existing fences and verbal agreements with neighbours are often not where the legal boundary runs, and even a small encroachment can lead to a demand to move the fence.</p>
<p>Taking down a run of aluminium panels, breaking out concrete footings and reinstalling everything on the correct line costs far more than checking first, and boundary disputes can sour relations with neighbours for years. In England and Wales, start with your HM Land Registry title plan (which usually shows only general boundaries), check your deeds, talk to your neighbour and bring in a boundary surveyor if there is any doubt. In the US, a professional property survey will mark your property pins. Our <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-mistakes">privacy fence mistakes</a> guide covers this process in more detail.</p>
<p>One aluminium-specific point: panels come in fixed widths, so plan the post positions along the agreed line before ordering, and set the fence slightly inside your boundary so posts and footings stay on your land.</p>

<h2 id="underground-hazards">2. Digging without checking for buried services</h2>
<p>Under most gardens there are water pipes, cables, telecoms lines and sometimes gas pipes. An aluminium fence needs a post hole every panel width, so there are plenty of chances to hit something. Damaging a service can be dangerous, and you may be liable for the repair.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>United States:</strong> call 811 before you dig so the utility companies can mark their lines.</li>
<li><strong>UK:</strong> ask the utility companies for plans of their apparatus near your property, scan the fence line with a cable avoidance tool and dig by hand near any marked route.</li>
</ul>
<p>Also check for easements (US) or wayleaves and rights of access (UK). If a utility company or a neighbour has the right to reach land your fence crosses, the fence may have to come down when they need access. Because aluminium panels lock into their posts, removing one section often means loosening the panels either side, so keep known access routes clear or plan a removable section or gate there.</p>

<h2 id="sloped-terrain">3. Ignoring the slope</h2>
<p>Not every garden is flat. A common mistake is buying standard flat panels and trying to fit them on a slope without a plan. Fixed panels on a slope leave triangular gaps under the bottom rail, which let pets and wildlife through, weaken security and spoil the clean lines that make aluminium fencing attractive. Fixing this later usually means ordering different panels or regrading the ground.</p>
<p>There are two good approaches, and you should choose before you order:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rackable panels:</strong> the rails tilt to follow the slope while the pickets stay vertical, so the bottom of the fence follows the ground. These suit gentle, even slopes. Check the manufacturer's stated maximum rack for the panel.</li>
<li><strong>Stepped installation:</strong> each panel stays level and drops in steps down the slope, like a staircase. This suits steeper or uneven ground, but leaves a gap under the low end of each panel that may need infilling or planting.</li>
</ul>

<!--figure:metal-fence-on-sloping-wall|On sloping ground, panels must be stepped or racked to follow the line of the land.-->

<h2 id="shallow-footings">4. Setting the posts too shallow</h2>
<p>An aluminium fence is only as stable as its posts. Because the material is light, some DIY installers assume deep footings are unnecessary, which is a costly error. In cold areas, frost heave can lift shallow posts as the ground freezes and thaws, and in any climate wind load can make shallow posts lean over time.</p>
<p>A leaning aluminium fence is awkward to fix because the panels are fixed into the posts; when one post moves, it pulls the panels either side out of line. As a guide, posts are commonly set around a third of the post length below ground, or as the manufacturer and local conditions require. Follow the fence manufacturer's installation guide for hole size, depth and concrete, and go deeper or wider where your installer advises on exposed sites or soft or frost-prone soil.</p>

<h2 id="cheap-materials">5. Buying the cheapest panels</h2>
<p>With aluminium fencing, quality varies a great deal between makers. Cheaper panels often have thinner walls and a weaker coating. They can dent and bend easily, and the finish can start "chalking", where the coating breaks down into a pale, powdery film after years of sun.</p>
<p>A heavier-gauge fence with a good-quality powder coating costs more upfront but usually stays straight and keeps its finish longer, so you are less likely to face early replacement. Choose a reputable manufacturer with a clear written warranty, read what it covers (such as peeling, cracking or chipping of the finish) and what it excludes, and keep the paperwork.</p>
<p>It also helps to know that aluminium does not rust the way iron and steel do, but it can corrode. Where the coating is scratched or chipped, the bare metal can develop white oxide, and this is more likely near the coast or where salt spray or de-icing salt reaches the fence. A good coating, prompt touch-ups and regular rinsing all help.</p>

<!--figure:decorative-metal-fence-scrollwork|Better panels have thicker rails, stronger welds and a more durable powder coat.-->

<h2 id="gate-installation">6. Treating the gate like another panel</h2>
<p>The gate is the only moving part of the fence and the part most likely to fail. A common mistake is hanging a gate on a standard line post. Over time the swinging and slamming make the post flex, the gate sags and the latch stops lining up. Fixing it often means digging out the post and starting again.</p>
<ul>
<li>Use heavier gate posts with thicker walls, as the manufacturer recommends for your gate width, set in larger, deeper footings than the line posts.</li>
<li>Choose adjustable hinges and a good latch, so you can make small corrections over time without reinstalling the gate.</li>
<li>If the fence encloses a swimming pool, the gate usually needs to be self-closing and self-latching, and there may be rules on fence height, gaps and latch height. Check the local regulations and the manufacturer's pool-barrier guidance before you buy.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="permit-neglect">7. Skipping planning, permit or HOA approval</h2>
<p>Owning the land does not mean you can build any fence you like. Most places have rules on fence height and position, and many have rules on materials and colour too.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>England:</strong> a fence is generally permitted development if it is no more than 2 m high, or no more than 1 m high next to a highway used by vehicles. Listed buildings, conservation areas, Article 4 directions and planning conditions can change that, so check with your local planning authority.</li>
<li><strong>United States:</strong> towns and HOAs often set rules on height, setbacks from the street and pavement, and even fence colour and style. Get any permit from your local building department and written HOA approval before you buy materials.</li>
</ul>
<p>The cost of checking is small compared with being told to lower or remove a finished fence. Our <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-mistakes">privacy fence mistakes</a> guide explains the planning side in more depth.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before ordering, sketch the fence line with every post, gate and change in level marked, then walk it with your installer. Panel widths, slope method and gate positions are far easier to change on paper than in concrete.</p></blockquote>

<!--figure:street-metal-fence-brick-building|Fences beside a road or footpath often need approval before they go up.-->

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does aluminium fencing rust?</h3>
<p>Not in the way iron and steel do, because aluminium contains no iron. It can still corrode, though: where the powder coating is damaged, the bare metal can form a white, powdery oxide, especially near the coast or where salt reaches the fence. Keep the coating intact, touch up chips promptly and rinse off salt regularly.</p>
<h3>Is it cheaper to install an aluminium fence myself?</h3>
<p>You save on labour, but DIY installs are where many of the mistakes above happen, such as the wrong panels for a slope or shallow footings. If the fence has to be reset or moved, the saving can disappear. If you do it yourself, follow the manufacturer's installation guide closely and get the boundary, services and approvals sorted first.</p>
<h3>What is the difference between residential and commercial grade aluminium?</h3>
<p>Commercial grade generally has thicker walls and larger pickets and rails, designed for heavier use or extra security. For most gardens a good residential grade is enough, but it is often worth upgrading gate posts to a heavier section.</p>
<h3>How long does an aluminium fence last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the quality of the panels and coating, the installation, your climate and how well it is looked after. Because the metal does not rot, the footings and the finish usually decide how long it stays looking good. Check the manufacturer's warranty for its stated term.</p>
<h3>Do I need to maintain an aluminium fence?</h3>
<p>Very little compared with timber: no staining or sealing, just gentle cleaning, a check of posts and gates, and touch-ups where the coating is chipped. See our <a href="/blog/aluminium-fence-maintenance-by-season">aluminium fence maintenance by season</a> guide for a full routine.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most costly aluminium fence problems are settled before the first post goes in: the right boundary, buried services checked, the right panels for the slope, solid footings, quality materials, strong gate posts and the necessary approvals. Get those right and the fence should stay straight and smart for many years. Once it is up, keep it in good shape with our <a href="/blog/aluminium-fence-maintenance-by-season">aluminium fence maintenance by season</a> guide, and if you are still weighing up options, read our <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-cost">privacy fence cost</a> guide. Explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your outdoor space with a designer.</p>
